## Method

Residata reads Slovak developers' price lists every day, flat by flat. This overview rests on {totalProjects} projects making up the supply in {towns} towns, and on observations since {windowFromEn}, that is {windowDays} days.

**What is counted.** *Available flats* are flats the developer lists as available; reserved and sold are excluded. Only projects whose price list we currently read enter the overview — a project we have stopped reading drops off the map along with its last known offer. A town is on the map if it has at least {mapMinStock} available flats; below that line these are individual leftover flats rather than a town's supply. Only homes are counted — garages, parking spaces, cellars and commercial units are excluded from every figure. The *median €/m²* is the median of individual flats' unit prices, computed on living area and including VAT; these are asking prices from price lists, not achieved prices from the land registry. Bencont and BuiltMind publish a mean and work with usable area, so their unit price and ours cannot be compared directly.

**How the largest project's share and the median shift are computed.** The largest project's share is its number of available flats divided by the town's available flats. The median shift is the difference between the town's median and the median of the same town once its largest priced project is removed; the shift is reported only where at least ten priced flats remain.

**How a price change is computed.** The price of the same flat in the same project is compared against its own earlier price, at one reading per flat per day and a change of at least two hundred euros. It is therefore a change on a unit, not a movement of an average.

**Coverage check.** The rezido.sk register lists {covIndexProjects} Slovak projects, {covSoldOut} of them sold out. A comparison against our catalogue on {asOfEn} found {covMissingProjects} selling projects we do not track, holding {covMissingUnits} available flats — {covMissingPctEn}% of the national supply.

**Check against published figures.** For Bratislava, where there is something to compare with, our data agrees with what the established houses publish: {benchSource} reports a supply of {benchSupply} flats for {benchPeriod}; on a comparable definition we get {ourSupplyComparable}, a difference of {supplyDeltaPctEn}%. Their average asking price per flat is {benchMeanPrice} € and ours {ourMeanPrice} €, a difference of {meanPriceDeltaPctEn}%.

**Scope of this issue.** It works with supply and prices only.

